{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"On node1, run a web application inside a chroot as the unprivileged appsvc account and serve it on TCP port 8080.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Direct process evidence before and after reboot shows UID/GID 997/995, NoNewPrivs enabled, process root /srv/app-chroot, a listener on 0.0.0.0:8080, and successful HTTP responses from both / and /health.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a8fc9f2715e04a24","cmd-8f8208e971a742ac","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Start the application automatically at boot without requiring an interactive login session.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The service was enabled and healthy before reboot. A bounded reboot changed the boot ID, after which the enabled service returned active with a new PID and successful HTTP responses. appsvc remained password-locked with /sbin/nologin, demonstrating that its startup did not depend on an interactive appsvc login.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a8fc9f2715e04a24","cmd-1b0b80dcbf774b78","cmd-c7b527555edb46ea","cmd-b10854b42ac74dc4","cmd-8f8208e971a742ac","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel, using software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The baseline and recovered system both report RHEL 7.9 and kernel 3.10.0-1160.el7.x86_64. The application uses the platform's Python 2.7.5 runtime and remained functional after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ca08f98d4a9143d4","cmd-b2cd0426ad654c74","cmd-a8fc9f2715e04a24","cmd-8f8208e971a742ac","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.86,"summary":"Final state was clean and functional: no failed services were recorded, and the new port-8080 listener was task-required. Two unnecessary unit directives were temporarily introduced in cmd-b2b3bafe19e74c33: WorkingDirectory=/app and PrivateTmp=yes caused failed restart loops and did not contribute to the final solution; both were fully reverted by cmd-0e6a0d4cf967495f and cmd-de67ef4361884fe0. The reboot procedure in cmd-1b0b80dcbf774b78 left /tmp/antrieb-reboot.log behind; this harmless temporary residue remained after completion. Other mutations in the trace have clear roles in constructing, correcting, enabling, testing, or reboot-verifying the requested service.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b2b3bafe19e74c33","cmd-ffb13706bf0f4002","cmd-0e6a0d4cf967495f","cmd-44bb11f2c4ba44e8","cmd-de67ef4361884fe0","cmd-1b0b80dcbf774b78","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The mandatory node-restart protocol is complete and coherent: healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement and observed outage, a different post-restart boot identity, and recovered service, account, firewall, chroot, listener, and HTTP state. Fixed lifecycle boot observations independently corroborate the reboot.","limitations":["Native package-manager consistency checks timed out in both lifecycle snapshots, so package database health was not independently established.","Lifecycle snapshots provide only bounded inventories, not a complete filesystem-difference audit; hygiene conclusions therefore rely on the complete command trace plus the captured temporary-file, failed-service, socket, platform, and boot observations."]}
